## 📱 COMPONENT MOBILE OPTIMIZATION BLUEPRINTS

> **CRITICAL CONTEXT — READ BEFORE EVERY IMPLEMENTATION SESSION**
>
> The screenshots shared alongside these plans are **motivational references and approach examples**, NOT blueprints to copy 100%.
>
> - Vercel has a theme switcher in their navbar. **We don't. Don't add one.**
> - Vercel's Ask AI prompt styling is their brand. **Ours is our brand (emerald green).**
> - Zoho has yellow indicator dots. **Ours will match our design system (emerald/cyan).**
>
> The screenshots answer **one question only**: *"How does a premium brand solve this same mobile layout problem?"*
> Take the **structural approach**. Apply it to **our existing components**. Never add new content.
>
> **Golden Rule**: If a component works on desktop, DO NOT touch its desktop classes. Only add `sm:`/`md:`/`lg:` responsive prefixes or conditional mobile logic to change rendering on screens ≤ 768px.

---

### 1. Navbar — Mobile Full-Screen Dropdown

**File**: `components/layout/Navbar.tsx`

**Desktop behavior (KEEP AS-IS)**:
The navbar renders a sticky header with a mega-menu dropdown that opens on hover/click. The hamburger `Menu` icon is hidden on `md:` and above.

**Problem on mobile**:
Clicking the hamburger currently opens a Shadcn `Sheet` that slides in from the RIGHT SIDE like a drawer. This covers the screen including the header, which forces logo duplication inside the drawer. It feels like a hidden sidebar, not a primary navigation.

**Mobile behavior to implement**:
- Replace the `Sheet` slide-from-right with a fixed full-screen overlay that drops DOWN from BELOW the sticky header (`top-16`, `inset-x-0`, `h-[calc(100dvh-4rem)]`).
- The hamburger `Menu` icon in the main header toggles to an `X` when open. No separate close button inside the menu.
- The logo, and all other header elements stay fully visible at the top at all times.
- Nav items render as a clean vertical list with accordion expand/collapse for items that have sub-sections (like "Solutions").
- Sub-items get distinct left-padding (`pl-8`) and slightly smaller text than parent items.
- No hover background boxes on touch — rely on clean text and padding for tap targets.
- Animate: `opacity 0→1` + `y -8→0` on open. Fast, subtle. Not a heavy slide.

**Reference**: Vercel.com mobile navbar (hamburger → full-screen overlay below sticky header, accordion for Products/Solutions/Resources).
> ⚠️ Vercel has "Sign Up" and "Log In" buttons at the top of their menu. We have different CTAs. Do not copy their button structure — use our existing `primaryCtaLabel` and `secondaryLinkLabel` props.

---

### 2. Ask AI Panel — Mobile Full-Screen Modal

**File**: `components/layout/AskAiPanel.tsx`

**Desktop behavior (KEEP AS-IS)**:
Clicking "Ask AI" in the navbar opens a `motion.aside` that slides in from the right as a ~400px wide panel. It has a header, scrollable chat area, and an input bar at the bottom.

**Problem on mobile**:
A 400px-wide sidebar on a 390px phone screen fills the entire viewport but still *animates* like a sidebar (slides from right). The heavy `bg-card` boxes around AI message bubbles feel bulky on small screens.

**Mobile behavior to implement**:
- On `sm` and below: change Framer Motion animation from `x: "100%"` to `y: "100%"` (slides UP from bottom like a native iOS/Android sheet).
- On `sm` and below: Remove the `border`, `shadow`, and `bg-card` from AI response message bubbles. Let the text breathe directly on the dark `bg-background`.
- On `sm` and below: Redesign the input area — instead of a small pill input + separate round button, use a taller rounded-rectangle container with the send `ArrowUp` button placed **inside** the right edge of the input box (like iMessage/WhatsApp).
- The `X` close button and "Ask AI" title in the header remain the same — no changes needed there.
- No new icons, no new buttons, no new features. Same AI, same suggestions, same chat — just a better mobile shell.

**Reference**: Vercel.com "Ask AI" panel on mobile (full-screen, minimal chat bubbles, integrated input box).
> ⚠️ Vercel shows Copy/Share/Trash icons in the header. We currently have only a Close button. Do not add new icons unless they already exist in our component.

---

### 3. TurboComparisonNew — Mobile Vertical Stack

**File**: `components/sections/TurboComparisonNew.tsx`

**Desktop behavior (KEEP AS-IS)**:
Two terminal-style "comparison" boxes side by side (Without ClassGrid | With ClassGrid OS). Wide canvas with animated content inside each terminal.

**Problem on mobile**:
- Left side: Two boxes are in `flex-row gap-12`, totaling ~450px on a 390px screen → overflow.
- Right side: Canvas shrinks to fit screen width, but internal text stays at 11px → unreadable.

**Mobile behavior to implement**:
- Left column wrapper: Change `flex-row` to `flex-col md:flex-row`. Boxes stack vertically, one by one. Gap: `gap-8 md:gap-12`.
- Right column canvas: On mobile, either allow the two overlapping terminal cards to stack vertically, OR use `scale()` transform to fit the canvas within `100vw` while keeping text readable.
- No new content. No new labels. The same two comparison states, just stacked instead of side-by-side.

**Reference**: Vercel's Turborepo comparison section on mobile (boxes stack vertically with clear visual separation).
> ⚠️ We are NOT adding "Turborepo" numbers or Vercel's specific text. We are using our existing ClassGrid comparison content.

---

### 4. PlatformAnimatedBeam — Mobile Responsive Scaling

**File**: `components/sections/PlatformAnimatedBeam.tsx`

**Desktop behavior (KEEP AS-IS)**:
A "Unified Education Stack" visualization with animated wire beams connecting institution nodes on the left to ClassGrid in the center and module nodes on the right. Side-by-side layout with wide gaps.

**Problem on mobile**:
- Left column has `min-w-[210px]`, right column has `min-w-[230px]`.
- Together with `gap-5`, the minimum width is ~455px → overflows on 390px screen.
- Beams break or misalign because the container squishes.

**Mobile behavior to implement**:
- Left column: `min-w-[150px] sm:min-w-[210px]`
- Right column: `min-w-[170px] sm:min-w-[230px]`
- Gap between columns: `gap-2 sm:gap-5`
- Card internal padding: `p-2 sm:p-4`
- Card text: `text-xs sm:text-sm` (labels inside the node cards)
- **Beam recalibration is automatic**: `AnimatedBeam` calculates paths from element center points, so making elements responsive will auto-redraw the wire paths correctly. No manual beam changes needed.
- Do NOT stack columns vertically — the beam animation requires side-by-side layout to look meaningful.

**Reference**: Vercel's "Connected" section on mobile (same wire animation, tighter gaps, smaller cards — side-by-side maintained).

---

### 5. IsometricStackSection — Mobile "Graphic First" Layout

**Files**: `components/sections/IsometricStackSection.tsx` + `components/sections/isometric-stack.css`

**Desktop behavior (KEEP AS-IS)**:
Split layout — text/content panel on the left, 3D isometric stack graphic on the right. Horizontal navigation dashes below the text to switch between layers. Scroll interception drives layer transitions.

**Problem on mobile**:
- Text appears ABOVE the graphic. On a phone, the user reads the text describing a layer before they've even seen the graphic they're describing. Confusing UX.
- Navigation dashes sit below the text, completely disconnected from the graphic they control.
- Mobile CSS currently disables all `transform` animations on the 3D layers (`transform: none !important`), making it non-interactive.

**Mobile behavior to implement**:
- **Step 1 — Flip layout order**: On mobile, the 3D graphic should appear ABOVE the text. Use `flex-col-reverse md:flex-row` or CSS `order` property to reverse the stacking without changing DOM structure for desktop.
- **Step 2 — Vertical layer indicator dots**: On mobile, replace the horizontal dashes navigation with vertical circular dots positioned absolutely to the RIGHT side of the 3D graphic container. Each dot aligns vertically with its corresponding 3D layer. Add a short horizontal connecting line from each dot pointing left toward the layer.
- **Step 3 — Re-enable subtle mobile interactions**: Remove `transform: none !important` from the mobile CSS overrides (or scope it to non-active layers). Tapping a vertical dot should lightly scale the active layer to give visual feedback that the tap worked.
- Dot and line color: Use our existing emerald/cyan design tokens — NOT Zoho's yellow.

**Reference**: Zoho's product stack section on mobile (3D graphic on top, text below, vertical aligned dots with connecting lines to each layer).
> ⚠️ Zoho's dots are yellow. Our dots will be emerald (`#10b981`) to match our design system. The structural approach is the reference — not the color.

---

### 6. StructuredContentPage — Mobile Dual-Drawer Navigation

**File**: `components/templates/StructuredContentPage.tsx`

**Desktop behavior (KEEP AS-IS)**:
3-column layout: Left sidebar (navigation tree) | Center (main content) | Right sidebar (Table of Contents / TOC).

**Problem on mobile**:
A 3-column layout is impossible on a phone. Currently, columns likely stack or get hidden, losing access to the navigation and TOC entirely.

**Mobile behavior to implement**:
- Add a **sticky secondary sub-header** that sits just below the main app navbar, visible only on mobile (`md:hidden`).
- Sub-header has two controls:
  - LEFT: `☰ Menu` button → opens a LEFT-side drawer containing the navigation tree.
  - RIGHT: `📄` (page/doc icon) button → opens a RIGHT-side drawer containing the TOC ("On this page").
- Each drawer slides in from its respective side with a dim backdrop overlay.
- Clicking the backdrop or a close button dismisses the active drawer.
- Desktop 3-column layout is completely untouched.

**Reference**: Vercel Docs on mobile (`vercel.com/docs` — "☰ Menu" on the left, page icon on the right in a sticky sub-header).

